Consider diffractive deep-inelastic electron–proton scattering, in which the proton remains intact (or dissociates into a low-mass system) and a rapidity gap separates it from the hadronic system X produced by the virtual photon. Assume Regge-style factorization so that the diffractive process is described by the exchange of a pomeron with a partonic structure: the diffractive structure function F_2^{D(2)}(β, Q^2) (integrated over the pomeron momentum fraction x_IP) is built from pomeron parton densities f_{q/IP}(β,Q^2) and f_{g/IP}(β,Q^2), where β is the momentum fraction of the struck parton inside the pomeron and Q^2 is the photon virtuality.

The gluon density of the pomeron, f_{g/IP}(β,Q^2), can be probed in three independent ways:
(a) from the Q^2 dependence (scaling violations) of F_2^{D(2)} via the DGLAP evolution equations;
(b) from event-shape variables (e.g. thrust, energy flow) of the diffractive final state X in deep-inelastic scattering, which are sensitive to the relative quark/gluon composition of the pomeron;
(c) from diffractive dijet photoproduction cross sections, where the gluon density enters at leading order through γ g → q q̄ subprocesses.

Question: Assuming standard Regge factorization and QCD factorization for the diffractive parton densities, do the three determinations (a), (b) and (c) of the pomeron's gluon content yield mutually consistent gluon distributions f_{g/IP}(β,Q^2) when the statistical precision of the underlying measurements is improved?

